# Cloudflare Pages response headers for DetectLab.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# This file is the Cloudflare twin of the single [[headers]] block of
# netlify.toml. It is NOT placed at the repository root: Netlify publishes "."
# and reads any _headers file it finds there, so a root-level copy would have
# changed the live Netlify site. tools/build-cloudflare-dist.mjs copies it into
# the Cloudflare build output directory instead, and netlify.toml is untouched.
#
# Scope notes, from the Cloudflare Pages documentation:
#   • _headers applies to STATIC ASSET responses only. It is never applied to a
#     response produced by a Pages Function — those set their own headers in
#     code (functions/_lib/pages-adapter.js translates the Netlify CDN
#     directives, so the 7-day tile pins are preserved).
#   • Rules are merged: a request matching several blocks inherits all of them,
#     and a header set twice is joined with a comma. The blocks below are
#     therefore written so that no header is ever set twice for one path.
#   • The file itself is not served as an asset.

# ── site-wide hardening (additive; Netlify sends no equivalent today) ────────
# Deliberately minimal. A Content-Security-Policy is NOT added: index.html and
# the layer modules carry inline scripts and styles and load tiles from dozens
# of allow-listed government hosts, so a CSP would break the existing frontend,
# which this migration must not touch. Permissions-Policy is not added either,
# because the app uses geolocation for the live-location button.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

# ── Google OAuth one-time PKCE callback ──────────────────────────────────────
# Byte-for-byte the [[headers]] block of netlify.toml: the callback URL carries
# a one-time code, so it must never be stored, never leak a referrer and never
# be indexed. Preserved exactly as required by the migration brief.
/google-auth-callback.html
  Cache-Control: no-store
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# ── the SAME callback, at its extensionless URL ──────────────────────────────
# wrangler.jsonc sets `assets.html_handling: "none"`, so Pages serves
# google-auth-callback.html at BOTH `/google-auth-callback.html` and
# `/google-auth-callback` as a direct 200 — no redirect in either direction
# (measured with `wrangler pages dev`; see the comment there for the full table).
#
# Both spellings must therefore carry the three headers. A `_headers` rule is
# matched against the URL that produced the response, and each of these URLs now
# produces its own response, so the block is repeated byte-for-byte. Keeping the
# extensionless spelling working matters: it is the URL the Cloudflare side has
# been serving, and any bookmark, shared link or in-flight OAuth attempt that
# lands there must not lose `no-store`.
#
# This is a Cloudflare-only configuration file: nothing in google-auth-callback.html,
# sw.js or js/pwa-google-auth.js is changed, and netlify.toml keeps its single
# block. The sign-in itself is unaffected — the page reads the credential from
# `location.search`/`location.hash` and correlates the attempt through same-origin
# localStorage, never through the URL (PWA_GOOGLE_SIGN_IN.md §1).
# CLOUDFLARE_DASHBOARD_CHECKLIST.md still requires a real Google sign-in on the
# *.pages.dev preview before any DNS is moved.
/google-auth-callback
  Cache-Control: no-store
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow

# ── PWA shell ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The service worker and the manifest must be revalidated on every navigation,
# otherwise an installed PWA keeps running an older sw.js (the file's own
# version comments exist precisely to force that replacement). "no-cache" means
# "store, but revalidate" — it does not disable the offline cache the service
# worker builds itself, and it matches what Netlify's default asset headers
# already achieve, so no PWA behaviour changes.
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache
